---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import snapshot from "../../public/data/snapshot.json";

const meta = snapshot as any;
const barDate = meta.stocks?.[0]?.bar_date ?? "unknown";

const title = "Methodology";
const description =
  "Exactly how the data on this site is produced: sources, formulas, thresholds, " +
  "update frequency, what is excluded and where the numbers can be wrong.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Reference / Methodology">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything published here is computed from public daily price data using
    standard formulas. This page states exactly how, including the parts that
    make the numbers less impressive than they could be made to look.
  </p>

  <h2>Data source and universe</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>source</code><span class="meta">public end-of-day data</span></div>
      <p>Daily open, high, low, close and volume for NSE-listed equities,
        adjusted for splits and bonuses. No intraday data, no order book, no
        derivatives.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>universe_scanned</code><span class="meta">{meta.universe_scanned} tickers</span></div>
      <p>The full watchlist the pipeline attempts each run.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>rows_computed</code><span class="meta">{meta.rows_computed}</span></div>
      <p>Tickers with enough history (250+ sessions) to compute a 200-day average.
        The rest are skipped rather than approximated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>rows_published</code><span class="meta">{meta.rows_published}</span></div>
      <p>Selected by {meta.selection}. Thinly traded stocks are excluded
        deliberately — their indicator values come from prices that barely
        represent transactions, and a handful of trades can set the close.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Indicators and their parameters</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    All standard, all verifiable against any other source. Exponential moving
    averages are used rather than simple ones, so figures will differ slightly
    from commentary quoting the "200 DMA", which usually means a simple average.
  </p>
  <div class="formula">RSI            Wilder's, 14 periods, on closing prices
ADX / DI+ / DI-  Wilder's, 14 periods
ATR            Wilder's, 14 periods
EMA            21, 50 and 200 periods
Volume average simple, 20 sessions
52-week high   highest high over the trailing 252 sessions</div>

  <h2>Published conditions</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Six conditions are evaluated per stock. The thresholds below are the
    <em>defaults</em> — the screener lets you change every one of them, and the
    conditions are recomputed in your browser from the raw values.
  </p>
  <div class="formula">above_200dma      close &gt; EMA200
ema21_above_50    EMA21 &gt; EMA50
rsi_in_band       35 &le; RSI(14) &le; 70
adx_trending      ADX(14) &ge; 18
volume_above_avg  volume &ge; 20-day average
near_52w_high     within 25% of the 52-week high</div>
  <p class="lede">
    These are round textbook values, chosen so a reader can verify them anywhere.
    They are deliberately <strong>not</strong> the output of an optimisation, and
    they are not tuned to make any result look good.
  </p>

  <h2>Update frequency</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Recomputed once per trading day after the close by an automated job, which
    then republishes the site. The current snapshot was generated
    {" "}{new Date(meta.generated_at).toLocaleString("en-IN")} from the
    {" "}<strong>{barDate}</strong> session. Every stock page and every table row
    carries the date of the bar it was computed from, so a stale value is visible
    rather than passed off as current.
  </p>

  <h2>Handling of missing and bad data</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>nulls_are_shown</code><span class="meta">never filled in</span></div>
      <p>A value that cannot be computed is published as null and rendered as
        "n/a" or a dash. Nothing is interpolated, estimated or carried forward.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>unknown_counts_as_not_met</code><span class="meta">conservative</span></div>
      <p>A condition whose inputs are missing counts as <em>not</em> satisfied,
        rather than being guessed either way.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>insufficient_history</code><span class="meta">excluded</span></div>
      <p>Under 250 sessions and the ticker is dropped entirely. A 200-day average
        computed from 120 days would be misleading.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    These are real and worth stating rather than burying.
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>end_of_day_only</code><span class="meta">not live</span></div>
      <p>Everything is computed after the close from daily bars. Nothing on this
        site is real-time, and it is not suitable for intraday use.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>survivorship_bias</code><span class="meta">unquantified</span></div>
      <p>The watchlist is built from currently listed companies. Firms that were
        delisted, merged away or went bankrupt are absent. Any historical
        statistic drawn from this universe is biased upward as a result, and this
        site cannot say by how much.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>corporate_actions</code><span class="meta">source-dependent</span></div>
      <p>Prices are split and bonus adjusted by the data source. Where an
        adjustment is missing or late, indicator values for that ticker will be
        wrong until it works through. If a figure looks impossible, it probably
        is — please <a href="/contact/">report it</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>indicators_describe_the_past</code><span class="meta">by construction</span></div>
      <p>Every value here is a function of prices that have already occurred.
        None of them forecast anything, and no combination of them does either.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>no_costs_modelled</code><span class="meta">relevant to any backtest</span></div>
      <p>Nothing on the public site simulates trading. Where returns are ever
        discussed, brokerage, STT, stamp duty and slippage materially reduce them
        — and on illiquid names slippage alone can exceed any edge. See
        <a href="/learn/backtesting/">backtesting</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    It does not publish buy or sell signals, entry prices, stop-losses or
    targets. It does not rank stocks by quality or assign a proprietary score.
    The count of conditions met is a tally at published thresholds, not a rating.
    It does not provide personalised investment advice — see the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    If a value looks wrong, include the ticker and the bar date shown on the
    page. Every row carries that date, which makes it straightforward to check
    against another source. Corrections are welcome and will be made.
    <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
